  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/a7c0dbc5a27bdb0da8b5e319c52a38af85c09a75e90c957e9f7be3822cdb6d90.png)\n\nWhich of the following diagrams depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/49911d2a6e8e7f8bb13524a604507db0b797b06d237665081b8823aeb596e2da.png)",
